'''''Chamaeleon gummifer''''', also known as '''distaff thistle''', '''piney thistle'''<ref name="webster">{{cite web |author=William Torrey Harris and F. Sturges Allen |date=1913 |title=Piney |url=https://www.websters1913.com/words/Piney |website=websters1913.com |location= |publisher=The Merriam Company |access-date=2025-01-12}}</ref> or '''stemless atractylis''', is a [[thistle]] in the ''[[Chamaeleon (plant)|Chamaeleon]]'' genus. Formerly, it was placed in the ''[[Atractylis]]'' genus.<ref name="IUCN">{{Cite journal | last1 = Rankou |first1 = Hassan | last2 = Taleb| first2 = Mohammed | last3 = Ouhammou | first3 = Ahmed | last4 = Martin| first4 = Gary| title = IUCN Red List of Threatened Species: Carlina gummifera| journal = IUCN Red List of Threatened Species| accessdate = 2021-04-08| date = 2014-01-27| url = https://www.iucnredlist.org/species/53785739/53798737}}</ref> It is native to the Mediterranean basin, where it can be found in various habitats, including cultivated- or uncultivated fields and forests.<ref name="ToM">{{Cite web| title = A thistle in the daisy family| work = Times of Malta| accessdate = 2021-04-07| url = https://timesofmalta.com/articles/view/A-thistle-in-the-daisy-family.627844 |author= Paul Portelli |date=13 Oct 2016}}</ref><ref name="IUCN"></ref> It is a [[perennial]] herb producing a stemless, pinkish flower. The plant has a history of use in folk medicine, but it is very toxic due to the presence of [[atractyloside]] and [[carboxyatractyloside]].<ref name="Dahamna2004">{{Cite journal| doi = 10.1016/j.jep.2004.11.025| issn = 0378-8741| volume = 97| issue = 2| pages = 175–181| last1 = Daniele| first1 = C.| last2 = Dahamna| first2 = S.| last3 = Firuzi| first3 = O.| last4 = Sekfali| first4 = N.| last5 = Saso| first5 = L.| last6 = Mazzanti| first6 = G.|display-authors=3| title = Atractylis gummifera L. poisoning: an ethnopharmacological review| journal = Journal of Ethnopharmacology| accessdate = 2021-04-07| date = 2005-02-28| pmid = 15707749| url = https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0378874104005756| url-access = subscription}}</ref>

== Description ==
[[File:Atractylis gummifera1.JPG|thumb|left|Two ''Chamaeleon gummifer'' plants showing inflorescence and dried-out leaves]]
''Chamaeleon gummifer'' is a perennial thistle with a long [[rhizome]] extending up to 40 cm and spiky leaves emanating from its center.<ref name="MaltaWildPlants">{{Cite web| last = Mifsud| first = Stephen| title = Atractylis gummifera (Stemless Atractylis) : MaltaWildPlants.com - the online Flora of the Maltese Islands. | format = Web Page| accessdate = 2021-04-09| date = 2002-08-23| url = http://www.maltawildplants.com/ASTR/Atractylis_gummifera.php}}</ref><ref name="Dahamna2004"></ref> A pinkish [[inflorescence]] grows in the center, seen as a [[Pseudanthium#Capitulum|capitulum]] consisting of many small threadlike flowers.<ref name="Dahamna2004"></ref> The inflorescence is surrounded by spiny [[bracts]].<ref name="MaltaWildPlants"></ref> Unusual compared to other thistles is the fact that the inflorescence of ''Chamaeleon gummifer'' does not grow on a stem. The ripe fruit of the plant may ooze a white or yellowish gummy latex produced by the rhizome.<ref name="Dahamna2004"></ref> ''Chamaeleon gummifer'' flowers in late summer or early autumn.<ref name="ToM"></ref>

== Distribution ==
''Chamaeleon gummifer'' can be found along the [[Mediterranean basin]], including Northern Africa, the Iberian Peninsula, Italy, Greece and Malta.<ref name="IUCN"></ref><ref name="ToM"></ref> It grows in various habitats and on various substrates, including in cultivated- or uncultivated fields, shrublands, forests and in rocklands.<ref name="IUCN"></ref> It is rated as [[Least Concern]] on the [[IUCN Red List]].<ref name="IUCN"></ref>

== Toxicity ==
The toxicity of ''Chamaeleon gummifer'' is thought to be caused by two related [[glycoside|glycosides]], [[atractyloside]] and [[carboxyatractyloside]].<ref name="Dahamna2004"></ref> In Morocco, ''Chamaeleon gummifer'' is a common cause of plant poisoning, with children being especially vulnerable to severe effects.<ref name="Achour2013">{{Cite journal| issn = 1020-3397| volume = 19| issue = 11| pages = 953–959| last1 = Achour| first1 = S.| last2 = Rhalem| first2 = N.| last3 = Elfakir| first3 = S.| last4 = Khattabi| first4 = A.| last5 = Nejjari| first5 = C.| last6 = Mokhtari| first6 = A.| last7 = Soulaymani| first7 = A.| last8 = Soulaymani| first8 = R.|display-authors=3| title = Prognostic factors of Atractylis gummifera L. poisoning, Morocco| journal = Eastern Mediterranean Health Journal| date = November 2013| doi = 10.26719/2013.19.11.953| pmid = 24673087 |url=https://www.emro.who.int/emhj-vol-19-2013/11/prognostic-factors-of-atractylis-gummifera-l-poisoning-morocco.html| doi-access = free| url-access = subscription}}</ref> Accidental ingestion of the plant can be due to confusion with the [[artichoke]] thistle, or due to use of its gummy discharge as a chewing gum.<ref name="Achour2013"></ref> Fatal cases of poisoning are common, even with treatment of symptoms.<ref name="Achour2013"></ref><ref name="Hamouda2004">{{Cite journal| issn = 0145-6296| volume = 46| issue = 3| pages = 144–146| last1 = Hamouda| first1 = C.| last2 = Hédhili| first2 = A.| last3 = Ben Salah| first3 = N.| last4 = Zhioua| first4 = M.| last5 = Amamou| first5 = M.|display-authors=3| title = A review of acute poisoning from Atractylis gummifera L| journal = Veterinary and Human Toxicology| date = June 2004| pmid = 15171492}}</ref> At least 98 fatalities occurred due to ''Chamaeleon gummifer'' poisoning in Morocco between 1981 and 2004, most of whom were children under the age of 16.<ref name="Hami2011">{{Cite journal| doi = 10.1007/s13149-010-0102-8| issn = 1961-9049| volume = 104| issue = 1| pages = 53–57| last1 = Hami| first1 = H.| last2 = Soulaymani| first2 = A.| last3 = Skalli| first3 = S.| last4 = Mokhtari| first4 = A.| last5 = Sefiani| first5 = H.| last6 = Soulaymani| first6 = R.|display-authors=3| title = Intoxication par Atractylis gummifera L. Données du centre antipoison et de pharmacovigilance du Maroc| journal = Bulletin de la Société de pathologie exotique| accessdate = 2021-04-09| date = 2011-02-01| pmid = 21243460| url = https://doi.org/10.1007/s13149-010-0102-8| url-access = subscription}}</ref> Severe cases may be associated with liver and kidney failure.<ref name="Georgiou1988">{{Cite journal| doi = 10.3109/15563658809038564| issn = 0731-3810| volume = 26| issue = 7| pages = 487–493| last1 = Georgiou| first1 = M.| last2 = D| first2 = Biol| last3 = Sianidou| first3 = L.| last4 = Hatzis| first4 = T.| last5 = Papadatos| first5 = J.| last6 = Koutselinis| first6 = A.|display-authors=3| title = Hepatotoxicity due to atractylis gummifera-L| journal = Journal of Toxicology: Clinical Toxicology| accessdate = 2021-04-07| date = 1988-01-01| url = https://doi.org/10.3109/15563658809038564| pmid = 3230599| url-access = subscription}}</ref><ref name="Hamouda2004"></ref> There is currently no specific treatment to poisoning by ''Chamaeleon gummifer''.<ref name="Dahamna2004"></ref>

